<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Izz al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">943–978</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Izz al-Dawla Bakhtiyar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Izz al-Dawla Bakhtiyar was the Buyid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 967 to 978, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a>. His weak rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> was undermined by conflict between Daylamite and Turkish troops, and a mutiny led by the Turkish commander Sabuktakin in 974 brought his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> into Iraq. Adud al-Dawla defeated and killed him at Qasr al-Jiss in 978 and took Iraq for himself.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Izz al-Dawla" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Izz_al-Dawla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
